                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "For me, Guild Wars 1 is one of the most interesting, and most unique, games ever made.\n\nAt its core, it's a co-op CRPG that's also a quasi-MMORPG, with one of the largest game worlds out there (though it's not a seamless world, it's divided into many instanced areas), and large storylines in the campaigns. It has a pretty flat progression style, so instead of constantly having numbers-go-up, you'll instead mostly be getting new options and tools to play around with. All content in the game is doable solo (with customizable and semi-controllable AI companions), though people warn some of it is actually pretty challenging to do in that way. The game is, especially when compared with modern RPGs, no push-over with difficulty.\n\nThe buildcrafting in this game feels like creating a deck in a well made cardgame (and the game designers did mention that Magic The Gathering was one of their inspirations). And the enemies you encounter in PvE, for the most part, are all decked out with the same skills that are accessible to players, and use the same systems. So stuff like disenchants, interrupts, covering, debuffs, and such are all relevant in PvE as well.\n\nI didn't play it as much as I wanted back at the time, as I mostly just focused on the PvP side of it and had little experience with the PvE campaigns. And I was reluctant to try Reforged as I was afraid that the game did not age gracefully (the fear of not being to press space bar to jump was looming heavy over me).\n\nWell, it actually aged just fine. And resisting the urge to wanting to jump over everything was easier than I expected. Kinda feels like playing an early 2000s bioware CRPG like NWN or Jade Empire, in that regard.\n\nThe graphics are just fine, the scenery is pretty (if limited, of course), and the characters model are still pretty decent. Animations are pretty simple, though.\n\nThe more I replay games from those years, the more I feel sure the mid-2000s were really the golden age for MMORPGs and MMORPG-adjacents like this one; I once thought it could be just nostalgia, but nah, those games still hold up.",
